  <h1>How FMLA Damages Work</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>The remedies framework under 29 U.S.C. § 2617</h2><p>FMLA provides three categories of relief: (1) compensatory damages (wages, benefits, and other compensation lost due to the violation); (2) liquidated damages equal to the compensatory award; and (3) equitable relief including reinstatement, promotion, or other appropriate relief. Attorney fees and costs are also recoverable by the prevailing plaintiff.</p>
  <h2>Compensatory / back pay damages</h2><p>All wages, benefits, and other compensation that would have been received but for the FMLA violation, less amounts actually earned after the violation. Benefits include health insurance premiums paid by the employee during the violation period, retirement contributions foregone, and the fair market value of other lost benefits.</p>
  <h2>Liquidated damages</h2><p>An additional amount equal to compensatory damages &mdash; automatically doubling the award &mdash; unless the employer proves good faith and reasonable grounds for the violation. This mirrors the ADEA&rsquo;s liquidated damages provision. Courts have interpreted the good faith defense narrowly: an employer with a functioning HR department generally cannot claim ignorance of FMLA requirements.</p>
  <h2>Reinstatement</h2><p>Return to the same position (same title, duties, pay, benefits, and working conditions) or an equivalent position. Reinstatement is a preferred remedy but is not always feasible. Courts deny reinstatement when the position was eliminated in a genuine reduction in force, the employee&rsquo;s conduct independently warranted termination, or the relationship has become too hostile.</p>
  <h2>Front pay</h2><p>When reinstatement is denied, front pay covers projected future lost earnings. The duration depends on the employee&rsquo;s age, experience, job market, and likelihood of finding comparable work. Front pay is an equitable remedy &mdash; awarded by the judge, not the jury &mdash; and is not subject to the liquidated damages doubling.</p>
